            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "A great recipe, but even better with some additions. I followed the other reviewers' advice, and used lemon extract instead of the vanilla. Then, I tinted the icing with yellow food coloring, and I also added vanilla and lemon extract (1/4 tsp each) to it. I drizzled the icing over the tops of the cookies and then sprinkled confectioner's sugar on top of the icing to pretty them up a bit. The result was a tasty cookie that was easy on the eye, and even more pleasing to the tummy! Will definitely try these again!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "BAZAARBAKER"
            },
            "reviewBody": "A variation of this recipe recently won top prize at a cookie exchange I attended. Fantastic cookies. The variation I have uses cream cheese frosting - 4 oz cream cheese, 1 Tbl butter, 2 C 10X sugar, and 1/2 tsp vanilla. It really adds to the wonderful flavor.",
            "datePublished": "2007-12-22T18:27:17.100Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "BBFORBIS"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These are wonderful cookies. They are light, fluffy and melt in you mouth wonderful. \n\nI made sure that I whipped the heck out of the butter and sugar in the first step (until it looked like whipped butter, about 5 minutes using a stand mixer) , this is very important for a successful cookie. \n\nThe other adjustment I made was to add about 1 1/2 Tablespoons of butter, 1/2 Tsp of vanilla. I happened to have on hand some Wilton icing white as well. Took advice from another review and dusted with powder sugar, and clear/silver sprinkles to finish. They look a wonderful as they taste.",
            "datePublished": "2017-12-24T18:22:58.850Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Gramminator"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I had made lasagna and had 7 oz of ricotta cheese left. So I substituted 8 oz sour cream. My grandkid's loved them. The recipe makes a large batch, so froze about half (unfrosted). When we needed more, I took the plain cookies out of the freezer and put the glaze on top. To top them I used glaze, powered sugar and sprinkles. The dough is a bit sticky, so next time I will try a cookie scoop with a trigger release. If you want a very smooth, melt in your mouth cookie, then this is your cookie.",
            "datePublished": "2018-05-10T20:55:55.967Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Rod Grabowski"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Having made these before (lost my original recipe); I remember one of the important steps to this recipe is to fold the ricotta in last so that you don't work it.\n\nI then topped with a great cream cheese frosting!",
            "datePublished": "2018-12-09T22:59:43.713Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CCooley"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These remind me of Mrs. Rannali's ricotta cookies...a neighbor from my childhood. I have made these twice. The second time I added the zest of one lemon and 1 T. of lemon juice. Very very good. Thank you for sharing Lindak.",
            "datePublished": "2018-09-01T22:46:59.453Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Lisa Nolan"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made these but I changed the amount of baking powder to two teaspoons instead of two tablespoons. It sounded like way too much. I’ve nevee seen that amount in any cookie recipe. They turned out great with 2 teaspoons.",
            "datePublished": "2018-12-23T01:36:39.587Z"
          },
